course: world
lesson: 94
title: Medicines, doctors and hospitals
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A medicine is a substance that helps the body fight an illness, eases pain or prevents a disease; the right amount, called the dose, helps, but too much can be dangerous.
explain: Medicines should only be taken when a trusted adult gives them, following the instructions from a doctor, a pharmacist or the label — some pills look like sweets but can make you very ill.
explain: Many people care for our health: family doctors find out what is wrong, nurses look after patients, pharmacists prepare and explain medicines, surgeons operate and dentists care for teeth.
explain: Doctors use tools to see inside us without cutting: a stethoscope to listen to the heart and lungs, a thermometer to check for fever and X-rays to see bones.
explain: Example: before a new medicine is sold, it is tested on many volunteers and compared with a dummy pill to make sure that it really works and is safe.
fact: A dose is the amount of medicine to take and how often.
fact: Too much of a medicine can be harmful, even if the right amount helps.
fact: Children should take medicine only from a trusted adult, following the instructions.
fact: Antibiotics kill bacteria but not viruses, such as the viruses that cause colds.
fact: A course of antibiotics should be finished as the doctor says, even when you feel better, because some bacteria may still be alive.
fact: A pharmacist prepares medicines and explains how to take them safely.
fact: A surgeon is a doctor who operates on the body.
fact: A stethoscope lets a doctor listen to the heart and lungs.
fact: X-rays pass through soft parts of the body but not through bones, so bones show up in an X-ray picture.
fact: An anaesthetic stops a patient feeling pain during an operation.
fact: A fever is a body temperature above about 38 degrees Celsius.
fact: New medicines are tested carefully on many volunteers before they are sold.
fact: Alexander Fleming discovered penicillin, the first widely used antibiotic, in 1928.
exercise: What is a dose? | The amount of medicine to take and how often | The name of the illness | The bottle the medicine comes in | The doctor who gives the medicine
exercise: Why must you never take extra medicine to get better faster? | Too much medicine can be harmful | Medicine stops working after one dose | Extra medicine has no effect at all | It makes the medicine taste bad
exercise: Who should give a child medicine? | A trusted adult following the instructions | Any friend at school | The child alone, whenever they feel like it | Anyone who has the same illness
exercise: You find pills that look like sweets. What should you do? | Not eat them and tell an adult | Taste just one to check what it is | Share them out with your friends | Hide them away in your pocket for later
exercise: What does a pharmacist do? | Prepares medicines and explains how to take them | Operates on patients | Fixes broken teeth | Drives the ambulance
exercise: What kind of doctor operates on the body? | A surgeon | A pharmacist | A vet | A dentist
exercise: What does a stethoscope do? | Lets the doctor listen to the heart and lungs | Takes a picture of the bones inside your chest | Measures how tall you are | Checks your blood for sugar
exercise: Why do bones show up on an X-ray? | X-rays pass through soft parts but not through bones | Bones glow in the dark | The X-ray paints the bones white | Bones give off their own light
exercise: What does an anaesthetic do? | Stops the patient feeling pain during an operation | Kills all the germs in the room | Makes the broken bones grow back together much faster | Cleans the blood after surgery
exercise: Which illnesses do antibiotics treat? | Illnesses caused by bacteria | Illnesses caused by viruses, like colds | Broken bones | All illnesses of every kind
exercise: Above about what temperature does a person have a fever? | About 38 degrees Celsius | About 30 degrees Celsius | About 35 degrees Celsius | About 50 degrees Celsius
exercise: Why are new medicines tested on many volunteers? | To check they really work and are safe | To use up all the spare medicine they made | To make them taste much nicer for children | To give the volunteers a nice free holiday
exercise: Who discovered penicillin? | Alexander Fleming | Florence Nightingale | Thomas Alva Edison | Charles Robert Darwin
exercise: What does a vaccine do that most medicines do not? | It prevents an illness before you catch it | It cures a broken bone | It works only after you are already very ill | It replaces sleep and food
test: Leo has a cold and asks for antibiotics. Why will the doctor probably say no? | Colds are caused by viruses, and antibiotics do not kill viruses | Antibiotics are only for adults | Colds are caused by bacteria that antibiotics cannot reach | Antibiotics make colds last forever
test: Which is true? | The right dose of a medicine helps but too much can harm | The more medicine you take, the faster you get well | Medicines that look like sweets are always safe | Any medicine can be shared with a friend
test: A girl falls and her wrist hurts badly. How can the doctor find out if a bone is broken without cutting? | By taking an X-ray | By listening with a stethoscope | By measuring her temperature | By giving her an antibiotic
test: In a medicine test, half the volunteers get the medicine and half get a dummy pill. Why? | To see whether the medicine works better than nothing | To save money on medicine | To make the test go faster | Because there was not enough medicine
test: Why does a patient feel no pain during an operation? | They are given an anaesthetic | The surgeon works very fast | Operations never hurt at all | They are given extra antibiotics
test: Your little brother finds a bottle of tablets in a bag. What is the safest thing to do? | Take it from him and give it to an adult | Let him taste one if it is small and round | Throw the bottle far out of the open window | Read the label and give him one small dose
test: A doctor puts a cold metal disc on your back and asks you to breathe deeply. What is she checking? | The sounds of your lungs | The strength of your bones | Your body temperature | How tall you are
test: Why should a course of antibiotics be finished as the doctor says, even when you feel better? | Some bacteria may still be alive and could come back | The medicine tastes better at the end | Leftover pills turn into vaccines | Feeling better means the medicine stopped working
test: A child's temperature is 36.8 degrees Celsius. What does it show? | A normal temperature | A high fever | Dangerous cold | That the thermometer is broken
test: why does the doctor listen to my chest with that thing | A stethoscope lets her hear your heart and lungs | It measures how tall you are | It checks your temperature by listening to your chest | It takes a picture of your bones
test: why do bones show up on an x-ray | X-rays pass through soft parts but not through bones | Bones glow in the dark by themselves | X-rays only see hard white things that are on the skin | The doctor paints the bones before the X-ray
test: Leo says if 1 spoon of medicine helps, 3 spoons'll help more. true? | No, too much medicine can be harmful | Yes, more medicine always works faster | Yes, but only with children's medicine | No, medicine never helps at all
test: my sister reckons antibiotics cure a cold. right? | No, antibiotics kill bacteria, and colds are caused by viruses | Yes, antibiotics cure every illness | Yes, but only if you take a lot | No, antibiotics only cure broken bones
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a dose? | The amount of medicine to take and how often | The name of the doctor who prescribes it to the patient | The place at which medicine is sold | The illness which a medicine treats
exam: Identify the organisms against which antibiotics are effective. | Bacteria | Viruses alone | All germs without exception | Fungi alone
exam: Ascertain the principal reason for which bones are visible in an X-ray picture. | X-rays pass through soft parts but not through bones | X-rays pass through bones but not through soft parts | Bones give off their own light | X-rays colour the bones white
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the discovery of penicillin. | Alexander Fleming discovered it in 1928 | Alexander Fleming discovered it in 1828 | Louis Pasteur discovered it in 1928 | It was discovered in ancient Egypt
